      "id": 1,
      "text": "Sherwood earned its name in 1891 when postmaster James Christopher chose it after Sherwood, Michigan, or the famed English forest.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2,
        7,
        20,
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sherwood%2C_Oregon",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The arrival of the Portland and Willamette Valley Railway in 1889 turned Sherwood into a rail hub, spurring rapid development.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11,
        12,
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sherwood%2C_Oregon",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Sherwood officially became a town in 1893 under Oregon Senate Bill 36, marking its first formal incorporation.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        19,
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sherwood%2C_Oregon",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "A pressed brick yard once dominated Sherwood’s economy in the 1890s, closing in 1896 after a national recession.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        27
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sherwood%2C_Oregon",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Sherwood Historical Society preserves the town’s past, offering exhibits that trace life from pioneer days to today.",
